0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124951 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124951 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36039" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124951 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124951 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/93/936cea1e20ef7bbc1eb66acf9dd564c61476f5bb68ee05e553b00aaf3f970dc3.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible? \n\n- $x\\in [-4,0]$\n- $x\\in (-4,-2]$\n- $x\\in (-3,1)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124951 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b8099f21-9137-5121-a075-a61a4c5ae84b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124951 #edb/ref 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n- The given function is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in [-4,0]$ and $x\\in (-4,-2].$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/a4/a4499c2d4995c09b9ab6f8852cc2982f415f1650d65429c86df83800344106f4.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval $x\\in (-3,1]$  because some lines will intersect the curve at more than one point.\n\n![](images/cb/cb0a462b3af10d15bb586f127baac45a74eecd5dc19848bda23664e75de968a4.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125322 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125322 #edb/ref 1061 13908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125322 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5b61f81e-5366-54d7-b4dc-eb12246fa126" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125322 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125323 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125323 #edb/ref 1061 13909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125323 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "619ae142-fadd-53d9-975f-a485d1f363b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125323 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125324 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125324 #edb/ref 1061 13911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125324 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "067f6188-2d0b-5a28-8513-b138cb5eda82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125324 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125325 #edb/ref 1061 13916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125325 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "956e822f-5a25-5d3b-ac84-ada9c2c8dee8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125325 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125368 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125368 #edb/ref 1061 189149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125368 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "de4015ef-1002-54f7-adee-e5eee4e87979" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125368 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125369 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125369 #edb/ref 1061 189150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125369 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1c0d5ba6-1e60-5d4d-b42e-60ba32609db6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125369 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125370 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125370 #edb/ref 1061 189153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125370 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b5810a9c-9d11-54cc-9534-f5807fd71ff5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125370 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125371 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125371 #edb/ref 1061 189156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125371 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "83088d79-842b-5477-8d05-56eb6c96e451" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125371 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125372 #edb/ref 1061 189159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125372 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5a457aae-70d6-5725-8620-6593b8838a70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125372 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125752 #edb/ref 1064 "A function is **invertible** on an interval if and only if it's one-to-one. In other words, it must pass the horizontal line test.\n\nNot all functions have inverses! For example, consider the function $f(x)=x^2-1$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/e9/e99832fa1867a8314dda20f392d0c897ebb2942ac91a652ab1cf474b5d2d9612.png)\n\nNotice that $f(x)$ is not a one-to-one since it does not satisfy the horizontal line test.\n\nTo find the graph of a function's inverse, we usually reflect it over the line $y=x.$ However, if we reflect $f(x)$ over the line $y=x,$ the result is not a function since it will not satisfy the vertical line test.\n\n![](images/7a/7a5a95f1cc3e2c8b40e865a24d22f4529e47c3c0137a6cb8d0369059f4a89a56.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is *not* invertible.\n\nIf a function is not invertible, we can sometimes make it invertible by restricting its domain.\n\nHere, if we take $f(x)=x^2-1$ over the restricted interval $x \\in (0,\\infty),$ then it passes the horizontal line test and we can find the corresponding inverse function by reflecting the graph of $y=f(x)$ over the line $y=x.$\n\n![](images/0a/0a7f98c2dce4599ee74cc13dc53c3a693b9c3716ffe54c708811f4c011f18b6d.png)\n\nTo determine if a function is invertible over a particular domain, we need to use the horizontal line test to check if it's a one-to-one function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125752 #edb/ref 1065 2768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125752 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "90b8b9a7-2b60-5a13-9829-c02575ad8c16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125752 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125899 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we have a straight line passing through the point $P(3,-1,7)$ and that's parallel to the direction vector ![](images/54/54405c5c6e2502e5174525c2ee36a99366a79933b47e316a09858f827fda8dae.svg) As we know, we can write the vector equation of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nUsing the vector equation above, we can create a set of **parametric equations** where we express a general point $(x,y,z)$ in terms of  the parameter $t.$\n\nIf we let ![](images/67/67b8360f90540ffb75d39717c375f0ef6e9a8a39cae5832c78957105e0d148b6.svg) be the position vector of the general point $(x,y,z),$ then the above equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1+5t \\\\ 7-3t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n![](images/64/64cf325014eff341673557321ef565e35988b836630f3fbeb52af8024d7aaad7.svg)\n\nThe equations in the above system are called the **parametric equations** of the straight line.\nGeometrically, the equation states that a point belongs to the line if and only if its coordinates have the form\n\n$$\n(3, \\: -1+5t, \\: 7-3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n![](images/e2/e29ce95cdccb47b97578f6431a96e608f7af9f6709410b221ff517b436896608.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125899 #edb/ref 1065 9660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125899 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6ff73cc8-91a0-5a25-9c14-6e87496adac8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125899 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126386 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126386 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126386 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126386 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126386 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126386 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126386 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126386 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "95824ee1-eaa5-5a70-8ab3-8de0d1f815bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126387 #edb/ref 1080 "t = 1s" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126387 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126387 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d039f4a6-471f-5a6d-8c7b-7b6f20da9885" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126388 #edb/ref 1080 "No solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126388 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126388 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d6fbc6ac-7bd1-59a8-ba1b-7358d4ca8319" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126389 #edb/ref 1080 "t = 3s" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126389 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126389 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ed75ad4e-24ff-5a53-9276-d9d4a0a10469" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126390 #edb/ref 1080 "t = 2s" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126390 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126390 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "408ad7c7-e164-5b6a-884f-a6633e2eeb97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126391 #edb/ref 1080 "t = 6s" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126391 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 1759218